    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and all its tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented E.164 telephone handling for owner creation. Two files changed:\n\n**`TelephoneNormalizer.java`** \u2014 rewrote `normalize()` to produce E.164:\n- Strips spaces, dashes, and brackets (`( ) [ ] { }`).\n- If the cleaned value starts with `+`, keeps the `+` and country code as-is.\n- Otherwise assumes country code `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits.\n- Requires the result to be a `+` followed by 8\u201315 digits, throwing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` (\u2192 HTTP 400) otherwise.\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 replaced the old bean-validation constraints (`@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")`) with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` so the persisted E.164 value passes validation on save. Removed the now-unused `@Digits` import.\n\nThe controller already normalized the telephone before storing and used the stored value for duplicate detection (`findOwnerByTelephone`), so storing/returning E.164 and comparing E.164 for duplicates follows automatically. The `telephone` column is `VARCHAR(20)`, which comfortably holds the max 16-char E.164 string.\n\nVerified against the tests:\n- `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` \u2713\n- `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456` \u2713\n- `12` \u2192 400 (would become `+6112`, only 4 digits) \u2713\n\nTest suite: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
